---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { clampText } from "../../lib/text.js";

const description = clampText(
  "Guides to NHTSA UTQG tire grades and to the DOT tire identification number, with links to the ratings table and the decoder.",
);
const crumbs = [
  { href: "/", label: "Home" },
  { href: "/guides/", label: "Guides" },
];
---

<Base title="Tire grade and DOT code guides" description={description} breadcrumbs={crumbs} narrow>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Guides</h1>
    <p>Two explainers for the data on this site. Both stick to the regulations and to NHTSA's own descriptions.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/guides/utqg-grades/">What UTQG treadwear, traction, and temperature grades mean</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/dot-code/">How to read a DOT tire identification number</a></li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Recall filings</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/who-makes-it/">Who makes it?</a> lists the company that filed each NHTSA tire recall since 2010.
      The filer may be the factory, an importer, or the brand owner. A brand with no tire recall in that file is not answered there.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/methodology/">data sources</a> page describes the files, including brand-string errors, plant status, and which tires UTQG does not cover.
      If a light-truck, winter, or temporary spare tire is missing from the ratings, start with <a href="/guides/utqg-grades/#not-in-the-list">why a tire may be absent</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
